  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to optimize the maximum overlap integral \\(C_5\\) of a step function \\(h(x)\\) on \\([0,2]\\) with integral 1. The key insight is to reduce the autocorrelation within the function, as \\(C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\\), which is equivalent to maximizing the minimum autocorrelation. A lower \\(C_5\\) is achieved by distributing the function's values evenly across the domain to minimize self-overlap at any shift. This approach avoids local optima by combining local search (GREEDY) with global exploration (PENALTY), leveraging exact sum and box constraints through repair mechanisms.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n- **Representation**: A binary step function \\(h(x)\\) discretized as a vector \\(h \\in \\{0,1\\}^{n_{points}}\\) with \\(\\sum h_i = n_{ones} = n_{points}/2\\).\n- **Objective**: Minimize \\(C_5 = \\max_k \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\"full\") \\times (2.0 / n_{points})\\).\n- **Constraints**: \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) and \\(\\sum h_i = n_{ones}\\).\n- **Variable**: \\(h\\) (decision vector), \\(n_{points}\\) (grid resolution, \\(n_{points} \\in \\{200,140,100\\}\\)).\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. **Input Handling**: \n   - `n_points` (from 200, 140 to 100), seed.\n   - `parent` (from `initial_h_values`, already satisfies constraints).\n2. **Initial Solution**:\n   - For `parent`, pad zeros to `n_points`.\n   - For others, use a regular pattern for even distribution.\n3. **GREEDY Local Search**:\n   - **Iterations**: 30,000 for 200 pts, others ~200s budget.\n   - **Operator**: Flip one bit, repair sum constraint.\n   - **Repetition**: 90 times per resolution.\n4. **PENALTY Optimization**:\n   - **Time**: Up to 200s per resolution.\n   - **Algorithm**: Mixed-integer optimization scaling a continuous relaxation.\n   - **Penalty**: Incorporate \\(\\sum h_i - n_{ones}}\\)into objective.\n   - **Repair**: At boundaries.\n5. **Termination**: Return best \\(h\\) found at any resolution.\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n- **Resolution Order**: 200, 140, 100.\n- **Repair**: Local repair by random bit flips until sum matches.\n- **Time Budget**: GREEDY runs consecutively across grids; PENALTY uses elapsed time.\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness:\n- **Operations**: \\(\\mathcal{O}(n_{points}^2)\\) per fitness eval.\n- **Scheduling**: GREEDY runs in 20~4000s; PENALTY adapts to runtime.\n- **Fallback**: Returns best solution from any resolution runtime.\n- **Validation**: Final repair ensures \\(\\sum h_i = n_{ones}\\) and \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\).\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n- **Feasibility**: Repair ensures sum \\(\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\) and bounds.\n- **Output**: \\((h_{200,140,100}) \\in \\{0,1\\}\\), \\(\\sum h_i \\equiv 0\\) mod 2, \\(C_5\\) minimized.\n\nHelpers:\n1. **repair_sum**: Flips bits until sum matches \\(n_{ones}\\).\n2. **compute_fitness**: Uses `np.correlate` then scales by dx.\n3. **GREEDY**: Dynamic repetition (10 or 90% time each resolution).\n4. **PENALTY**: Parameterized by \\(\\beta, \\gamma, \\mu\\) for constraint weight and box handling.",
